Here is the Terraform HCL program that configures an Elastic Beanstalk environment linked to a relational database instance, with the specified resource names and dependencies:

```hcl
provider "aws" {
  region = "us-west-2"
}

resource "aws_iam_role" "eb_ec2_profile" {
  name = "eb_ec2_profile"
  assume_role_policy = jsonencode({
    Version = "2012-10-17"
    Statement = [{
      Effect = "Allow"
      Principal = {
        Service = "elasticbeanstalk.amazonaws.com"
      }
      Action = "sts:AssumeRole"
    }]
  })
}

resource "aws_db_instance" "default" {
  instance_class = "db.t3.micro"
  engine         = "mysql"
  engine_version = "8.0.33"
  db_name        = "mydb"
  allocated_storage = 20
  username       = "admin"
  password       = "SecurePassword123!"
  auto_minor_version_upgrade = true
  skip_final_snapshot        = true
}

resource "aws_elasticbeanstalk_environment" "eb_env" {
  name                = "my-eb-env"
  application         = "my-app"
  environment_name    = "my-env"
  solution_stack_name = "64bit Amazon Linux 2023 v3.3.10 running Python 3.10"
  instance_profile    = aws_iam_role.eb_ec2_profile.arn
  database_instances  = [aws_db_instance.default.id]
}
```

### Description:
This Terraform configuration sets up the following infrastructure:

1. **AWS IAM Role (`eb_ec2_profile`)**: An IAM role that allows Elastic Beanstalk to assume it for managing EC2 instances. It is configured with a trust policy that allows the Elastic Beanstalk service to assume the role.

2. **AWS RDS Instance (`default`)**: A MySQL database instance with a specified version, storage, and credentials. It is configured with minimal settings to ensure it is functional and ready for use.

3. **Elastic Beanstalk Environment (`eb_env`)**: An Elastic Beanstalk environment that is linked to the database instance and uses the IAM role for EC2 instances. The environment is configured with a sample solution stack and environment name.

This configuration ensures that the Elastic Beanstalk environment is properly linked to the database instance and that the EC2 instances in the environment have the appropriate IAM permissions.

